//!
//! # vaani-diarization
//!
//! Speaker diarization for VaaniDesk: which speaker is talking, when, and
//! which transcript segment belongs to which person.
//!
//! ## What this is, stated plainly
//!
//! **This is a heuristic baseline, not a trained speaker embedder.** There are
//! no diarization model weights anywhere in this repository — `artifacts/`
//! contains SraVaani's ASR graphs and nothing else — so this crate computes a
//! hand-built spectral descriptor and clusters it. It is a working, complete,
//! self-contained diarization engine that produces usable speaker labels on a
//! meeting recording, and it is **not** ECAPA-TDNN or pyannote and claims none
//! of their accuracy. No diarization error rate appears anywhere in this crate
//! or in its tests against real audio, because there is no annotated real
//! reference to measure against. The one place a clustering error rate *is*
//! reported is [`synth`], where the reference is generated by the same call
//! that generated the audio, and even there it measures the engine, not the
//! world.
//!
//! A neural path exists in [`embed`] behind an availability check, for when a
//! speaker embedder is dropped into `artifacts/diarization/`. It compiles and
//! has never run a forward pass, because the weights do not exist. It is
//! documented as an interface contract, not offered as a feature.
//!
//! ## Module map
//!
//! | module | owns | must not know about |
//! |---|---|---|
//! | [`config`] | every number that changes the output, named | the signal |
//! | [`dsp`] | framing, spectra, cepstra, pitch, activity statistics | speakers, ONNX |
//! | [`features`] | the feature vector, its layout and normalisation | the pipeline |
//! | [`vad`] | the fallback activity detector | `vaani-vad` |
//! | [`cluster`] | agglomerative linkage, stopping rules, refinement | time, spans |
//! | [`smooth`] | median filtering, minimum turns, span assembly | audio |
//! | [`embed`] | the unexercised `ort` embedder adapter | the rest of the engine |
//! | [`attribution`] | mapping spans onto `Transcript` segments | the signal |
//! | [`engine`] | the `DiarizationEngine` impl and the pipeline | — |
//! | [`synth`] | synthetic voices and turn timelines | the engine |
//! | [`bench`] | the JSON benchmark harness and its label-free diagnostics | the product |
//! | [`wav`] | a minimal RIFF reader, for probes only | everything |
//!
//! ## The benchmark
//!
//! `cargo run -p vaani-diarization --release --bin vaani-diar-bench -- --all`
//! produces one JSON document of **structure and timing**: speaker count, spans,
//! speaker changes, wall time, RTF and the per-stage breakdown. It reports **no
//! accuracy for any real recording**, because this repository has no annotated
//! reference for real audio; the only scored cases are generated ones, whose
//! reference is the generator's own per-frame labels. See [`bench`].
//!
//! ## The two contracts that matter
//!
//! 1. **Times are absolute, and the frame geometry matches the ASR front
//!    end.** A span's times are in source-media coordinates, not chunk-local,
//!    and the 400-sample analysis window is *centred* at offset 56 in the
//!    512-point frame. That centring is the bug the ASR team paid for; getting
//!    it wrong here would shift every diarization span by 3.5 ms and produce
//!    output that still looks correct. See [`dsp`].
//! 2. **Diarization never depends on ASR output, and never depends on VAD
//!    output either.** [`engine::HeuristicDiarizer::diarize_with_timeline`]
//!    accepts a `SpeechTimeline` and prefers it, because the pipeline already
//!    computed one, but it works without one. Attribution is a separate call
//!    that takes a finished `DiarizationResult`, so a diarization model can be
//!    swapped or turned off without touching a line of ASR code.
//!
//! ## Product decisions encoded here
//!
//! * **Over-split is favoured over over-merge**
//!   ([`SegmentationBias::SplitEasily`]). An over-merge labels one person's
//!   words as another's and nothing in the UI says the label is suspect; an
//!   over-split shows one person as two and the user can merge two labels with
//!   a click. The bias is a setting, not a constant.
//! * **"No speaker" is a complete answer.** `Segment::speaker` is `None`
//!   whenever no span overlaps, and so is the whole result for a silent file.
//!   Nothing in this crate turns that into an error, and nothing panics on a
//!   span that names an unregistered speaker.
//! * **Spans are non-overlapping and tight.** The core type permits overlap,
//!   but overlap has no use once boundaries are decided, and overlapping spans
//!   would make the label at a boundary depend on span length.
//!
//! ## Verification
//!
//! * Unit tests live next to every pure function: framing geometry, mel filter
//!   bank, cepstra, pitch accuracy, percentiles, median reduction,
//!   normalisation, the `MinSpread` floor, linkage, the stopping rules, the
//!   min-speech guard, median filtering, turn absorption, span validation and
//!   attribution.
//! * `tests/synthetic_clustering.rs` synthesises interleaved speakers with
//!   known identity and asserts the recovered cluster count, purity and
//!   complete-link separation. This is the end-to-end correctness test.
//! * `tests/adversarial.rs` covers one speaker, silence, white noise, a 5 ms
//!   click, 8 kHz input and a 3-hour duration carrying 10 s of audio.
//! * `tests/properties.rs` asserts the structural invariants on every one of
//!   those results.
//! * `tests/cancellation.rs` measures the latency from `cancel()` to `Err`.
//! * `tests/real_clip.rs` runs the real 13-minute meeting and reports only
//!   aggregate numbers. It skips with a printed reason when `scratch/clip.wav`
//!   is absent, and it never copies, commits or prints any of the audio's
//!   content.

